%N A398512 n is the start of a run of exactly a(n) consecutive numbers all containing a repeated digit.
%C A398512 More than the usual number of terms are shown in order to distinguish this from many other sequences that begin with mostly 0's and 1's.
%C A398512 The sequence stops with a(9876543210)=0 because a(9876543211)=infinity.
%H A398512 James C. McMahon, <a href="/A398512/b398512.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%e A398512 a(99) = 3, since 99, 100, and 101 all have repeated digits (but 98 and 102 do not).
%t A398512 a[n_]:=Module[{c=0,nn=n},While[!DuplicateFreeQ[IntegerDigits[nn]],c++;nn++];c];Array[a,124,0] (* _James C. McMahon_, Aug 11 2026 *)
%Y A398512 For RECORDS see A398513, A398514, A398515.
%K A398512 nonn,base,fini
%O A398512 0,100
%A A398512 _N. J. A. Sloane_, Aug 10 2026
